@* User Guide — Review & governance: Documentation Drift and readiness. *@
@using BirkNext.Web.Services
@using static BirkNext.Web.Components.Guide.GuideCallout

<section id="documentation-drift" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="documentation-drift" aria-labelledby="documentation-drift-title">
    <h2 id="documentation-drift-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Documentation Drift</h2>
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/documentation-drift">Documentation Drift</a> compares documentation artifacts of an imported project: what the team intended (authored) against what was generated from the code, and how documents changed between imports.</p>
    <div class="ug-card-grid">
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/documentation-drift?tab=overview">Overview</a></h3><p>Counts per status and <em>What to review next</em>, scoped to the selected import.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/documentation-drift?tab=compare">Compare</a></h3><p>Authored intent ↔ generated documentation for one module and documentation area. Low compatibility offers <em>Compare anyway</em>; it never blocks.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/documentation-drift?tab=changes">Changes</a></h3><p>Revisions of the same document lineage across imports of the <strong>same</strong> project. With one import there is nothing to compare yet — import another version.</p></article>
        <article class="ug-card"><h3><a href="/documentation-drift?tab=drift">Drift</a></h3><p>An inventory of modules and areas with one status each, searchable and filterable, evidence collapsed, and a link to resolve a missing side.</p></article>
    </div>

    <h3 id="drift-statuses" class="ug-subtitle">Drift statuses</h3>
    <div class="ug-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Documentation Drift statuses" tabindex="0"><table class="ug-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-ready">Consistent</span></th><td>A saved comparison found no difference.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-warning">Difference</span></th><td>A saved comparison found items that differ.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-critical">Conflict</span></th><td>Authored documents disagree with each other, so no conclusion is drawn.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-warning">Review required</span></th><td>A comparison needs a person’s decision.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-info">Ready to compare</span></th><td>Both sides exist; nobody has compared them yet.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-neutral">Generated missing / Authored missing</span></th><td>Only one side exists. In comparison rows the same idea reads <em>Authored-only</em> or <em>Generated-only</em>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><span class="status-chip status-chip-neutral">Not comparable</span></th><td>The two sides cannot be compared meaningfully.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <GuideCallout Kind="CalloutKind.Important"><p>Missing evidence is not a difference. A module with only authored or only generated documentation is shown neutrally, with the action that adds the missing side.</p></GuideCallout>
</section>

<section id="readiness" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="readiness" aria-labelledby="readiness-title">
    <h2 id="readiness-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Readiness and review decisions</h2>
    <p class="ug-lead">Readiness answers one question per dimension — can this activity proceed? — with an owner and one next action for every gap. It is never a quality score.</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Testability first:</strong> readiness names what blocks a test (missing evidence, configuration, Test Data, credentials) and who owns it — Test Data, Target Environment, Integration Catalog, Source Analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>Blockers before review items:</strong> pages list blockers first; review items never stop preparation on their own.</li>
        <li><strong>Per activity:</strong> manual readiness, automated execution readiness, setup apply readiness and test readiness are separate answers.</li>
        <li><strong>Decisions are explicit:</strong> review decisions are recorded per artifact revision (see <a href="@UserGuideContent.Href("review-decisions")">Review decisions</a>); BirkNext never decides release readiness for you.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="delivery-overview" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="delivery-overview" aria-labelledby="delivery-overview-title">
    <h2 id="delivery-overview-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Delivery overview</h2>
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/delivery-overview">Delivery Overview</a> is a virtual SDD backlog of a delivery project: every feature with its user stories, acceptance criteria, phases and tasks, and one delivery status, from the newest frozen weekly report (or, without a report, the newest imported snapshot).</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Statuses stay separate.</strong> <em>Planned</em> (not started), <em>In progress</em>, <em>Implemented</em> (all tasks checked; ready for test and testing are shown as such), <em>Verified</em>, <em>Accepted</em> (an explicit “Status: Done” in the feature documents) and <em>Blocked</em> (explicit only). <em>Verified</em> needs verification evidence that snapshots do not contain, so it is shown as not available rather than inferred; checked tasks never make a feature Verified or Accepted.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> each row names the snapshot it comes from — a ZIP, or Azure DevOps project / repository / branch · commit (read-only). The baseline comparison of the report shows what changed this period; open the weekly report for details.</li>
        <li><strong>Workspace View</strong> (top of the sidebar: Project Leader, Scrum Master, Test Lead, Technical Reviewer, All Modules) only changes which groups come first and are expanded, and where it lands. It is a navigation preference, not a permission: every module stays available (collapsed groups, the sidebar search — also by former names such as “Implementation Review” or “Schema Studio” — and All Modules). Changing it never changes the selected project, and selecting a project never changes it.</li>
        <li><strong>Activity</strong> (top right of every page) lists imports, report generation, exports and Azure DevOps snapshot downloads. They continue while you move between pages, Workspace Views and projects; each keeps the project, repository, branch and baseline it started with. Cancel is explicit. A completed export can be downloaded again from Activity. Reloading or closing the browser tab ends what runs in that tab.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="delivery-reporting" class="ug-section ug-topic" data-topic="delivery-reporting" aria-labelledby="delivery-reporting-title">
    <h2 id="delivery-reporting-title" class="ug-section-title" tabindex="-1">Delivery reporting</h2>
    <p class="ug-lead"><a href="/delivery-reporting">Delivery Reports</a> answer “what changed in the project this week?” for a Scrum Master or Project Leader: which stories progressed, which are new, completed or need attention, and what to focus on next week.</p>
    <p>It is standalone: create a reporting project (for example M2LB), import a <strong>baseline</strong> and a <strong>current</strong> repository ZIP, then generate the weekly report. It has its own import and its own snapshots; it never uses Import Project, Source Analysis snapshots, Target Environment or Test Data. Each snapshot has an <strong>effective date</strong> (when the repository was in that state) separate from its import date, so a Git export of an earlier commit can be imported later as the baseline.</p>
    <ul class="ug-list">
        <li><strong>Feature → Phase → User Story → Acceptance Criteria.</strong> A Spec-Kit feature folder (<code>specs/&lt;feature&gt;/</code>) is the <em>Feature</em>; its <code>spec.md</code> sections “User Story N – …” are its <em>User Stories</em>, and acceptance scenarios written under a story belong to that story (criteria outside every story, such as success criteria, stay feature-level). A <em>Phase</em> exists only when <code>tasks.md</code> groups tasks under “Phase N …”; a phase is a delivery grouping, never a status. A feature without user story sections is still a valid feature.</li>
        <li><strong>Tasks</strong> always belong to their feature, to a phase when they sit in a Phase N group, and to a user story only through an explicit <code>[USn]</code> tag — never by similar wording. Task progress comes from <code>tasks.md</code> checkboxes.</li>
        <li><strong>Completed this period</strong> counts only tasks that were open in the baseline and are checked now. Tasks that appear already checked (in a new feature or as new tasks) are inventory growth, not work completed this week.</li>
        <li><strong>Stories</strong> tab: features grouped by module, changed and needing attention first; <em>View feature</em> opens Overview, Phases (when explicit), User Stories, Acceptance Criteria (Added / Changed / Removed, with the baseline text only when changed), Tasks, Changes and Evidence. Scope changes (new and removed features) are listed apart from Needs attention.</li>
        <li><strong>Tasks complete is not Done.</strong> Ready for test, Testing, Blocked and Done are shown only when the feature documents state them explicitly; open clarification markers give Needs clarification.</li>
        <li><strong>Removed is not completed:</strong> a feature missing from the current snapshot is a scope change to confirm, shown with its last known baseline state. Source changes are supporting evidence only. <em>Potential focus</em> lists observations, never an invented priority.</li>
        <li><strong>Snapshots imported earlier</strong> keep their meaning: their hierarchy is not re-parsed; re-import the archive to see user stories and acceptance criteria.</li>
        <li><strong>Two import paths, one parser.</strong> <em>Upload ZIP</em> (the default) reads an archive you exported. <em>Import from Azure DevOps</em> chooses a connection, project, repository and branch, then <em>Current / latest</em> or a past date and time: BirkNext resolves the latest commit on that branch at or before that time (never a later one), shows requested vs commit time, downloads that commit's tree and previews it exactly like a ZIP. The effective date is filled from the requested time; change it if needed. The same commit already imported into the project asks for confirmation. Snapshots from different repositories or branches are flagged in the report coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>Azure DevOps credentials.</strong> Create a personal access token with the scope <strong>Code (Read)</strong> only. Enter it in the connection form: it is stored encrypted on the BirkNext server, shown afterwards only as “Credential: Configured” and replaced by entering a new one. Never put a token in <code>appsettings.json</code>, <code>appsettings.Local.json</code> or a repository. BirkNext only reads from Azure DevOps; opening the page never contacts it — <em>Validate</em> and the lists do.</li>
        <li><strong>Reports are frozen:</strong> report history keeps every generated report exactly as generated, and Markdown/HTML exports always reproduce that version.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
